# clang-tidy configuration.
#
# The rule for what goes in here: a check earns its place if a finding from it
# would be worth a code review comment. Everything else is noise, and a linter
# that emits noise gets ignored and then disabled, which is worse than not
# having one.
#
# So this is a bug finder, not a style enforcer. Style is clang-format's job and
# it already runs in CI pinned to 18.1.8. clang-tidy is pinned to the same
# release for the same reason: the check set changes between versions and an
# unpinned linter turns an unrelated PR red.
#
# Run it with scripts/tidy.sh.

Checks: >
  -*,
  bugprone-*,
  cert-*,
  clang-analyzer-*,
  concurrency-*,
  misc-*,
  performance-*,
  portability-*,
  readability-const-return-type,
  readability-misleading-indentation,
  readability-redundant-control-flow,
  readability-simplify-boolean-expr,
  -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,
  -bugprone-unchecked-optional-access,
  -cert-err58-cpp,
  -misc-include-cleaner,
  -misc-non-private-member-variables-in-classes,
  -misc-no-recursion,
  -misc-use-anonymous-namespace,
  -performance-no-int-to-ptr

# Every disable above, and why. If you turn one back on, delete its entry.
#
# There is exactly one NOLINT in the tree, on bugprone-branch-clone in
# test/matcher.h, and the reasoning is at the line. It is a NOLINT and not an
# entry here because it is one deliberate instance rather than a check that
# does not fit this codebase, and the check should keep firing everywhere else.
#
# bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters
#   Three findings and every one of them is a real API smell:
#   PatternIndexType and IsPreviousLiteral each take two adjacent u32 or u16,
#   and insertAndUnlock takes three. Nothing the linter can do about it. The
#   fix is either strong typedefs or a parameter struct, and that is a change
#   to the public interface of the library, not something to slip into the
#   commit that turns a linter on. Worth doing, worth its own PR.
#
# bugprone-unchecked-optional-access
#   Three findings, all in skeleton.h, and all three are the same false
#   positive. The pattern is a nested loop:
#
#     while (auto seg = tok.NextSegment()) {
#       while (auto lit = tok.NextLiteral(*seg)) { ... }
#     }
#
#   NextLiteral takes `const LiteralSpan &`, see tokenizer.h, so it cannot
#   reset seg, and seg is not assigned anywhere in the body. The analyser drops
#   the has-value fact across the inner loop's back edge and reports the
#   dereference on the second iteration. Suppressing three lines with NOLINT
#   would be lying about three separate places instead of once here, and the
#   check has no way to be right about this shape.
#
# cert-err58-cpp
#   Two findings, both on the thread_local generators in common/rand.cc. The
#   check is about a throwing initialiser for an object constructed before
#   main, and it is technically correct. It is also unactionable without moving
#   to lazy construction behind a function, which costs a branch on every call
#   into the hot random path for a failure mode that is an allocation failure at
#   startup.
#
# misc-include-cleaner
#   Around fifty findings and most of them are a demand to directly include the
#   header that defines u8, u32, u64 or TupleID, which are this project's own
#   typedefs arriving through this project's own headers. Acting on those would
#   mean fifty new includes that say nothing. It is not all noise: util.cc
#   really does include filesystem, fstream, iostream, numeric, ranges, span,
#   string_view, vector, sys/stat.h and fmt/format.h and use none of them
#   directly. That is a concrete cleanup and it is filed as its own issue
#   rather than mixed into a CI change.
#
# misc-non-private-member-variables-in-classes
#   Thirteen findings, all of them fields of the ART node types, which are
#   plain aggregates with protected fields on purpose. Tree is a friend and
#   reaches them directly.
#
# misc-no-recursion
#   One finding, N256::deleteChildren. Freeing a tree by walking it is what
#   the check is complaining about, and the alternative is an explicit stack
#   for no benefit.
#
# misc-use-anonymous-namespace
#   Three findings, two in common/rand.cc and one in skeleton.cc, all asking
#   for `static` at file scope to be an anonymous namespace instead. Both give
#   internal linkage and the check is a preference between two spellings of the
#   same thing. The codebase uses `static` consistently and swapping it under a
#   CI change would be a diff nobody asked for.
#
# performance-no-int-to-ptr
#   Two findings, both in getLeaf and setLeaf, which are the tagged pointer:
#   a leaf is stored in the children array with the high bit set so a node
#   pointer and a leaf pointer can share one slot. That is the representation,
#   not an accident, and there is no version of it that does not cast an
#   integer to a pointer. The check is right that it costs the optimiser
#   something and that is the trade the design already made.

# Ours only. Everything left out of this list is somebody else's code and we do
# not get to lint it:
#
#   common/flat_map.h    ankerl::unordered_dense 4.8.1, vendored
#   common/perf_event.h  Viktor Leis's perf wrapper, vendored
#   common/utf8.h        carries an Umbra copyright, see issue #7
#   third_party/         croaring
#
# An allow list and not a deny list, because 18.1.8 has no
# ExcludeHeaderFilterRegex and a deny list expressed as a negative lookahead is
# unreadable. When a header is added under common/ it has to be added here too,
# which is the correct default for a directory that already holds three files
# nobody in this group wrote.
HeaderFilterRegex: 'src/include/(aho_corasick|art|kmp)/[^/]+\.h$|src/include/common/(constant|lrucache|rand|typedef|util)\.h$|test/[^/]+\.h$'

WarningsAsErrors: '*'

FormatStyle: file
